# Lab 12 – Ethics, Fairness, and Bias in Machine Learning

## Objective

The objective of this lab was to understand how machine learning models can inherit bias from training data and how to evaluate models for fairness using subgroup performance metrics. The lab also explored the ethical responsibilities involved in developing and deploying AI systems.

## What I Did

In this lab, I examined how biased data can influence machine learning predictions and evaluated a classification model for fairness by comparing its performance across different demographic groups. I calculated subgroup accuracy, analyzed confusion matrices, and compared metrics such as **False Positive Rate (FPR)** and **False Negative Rate (FNR)** to identify potential disparities. I also completed reflection questions about the ethical implications of bias in machine learning.

## What I Learned

This lab showed me that a model can achieve high overall accuracy while still performing unfairly for certain groups. I learned the importance of auditing machine learning models using fairness metrics instead of relying solely on overall accuracy. I also gained a better understanding of the ethical considerations involved in developing responsible and trustworthy AI systems.

## Skills & Tools

* Python
* Jupyter Notebook
* Pandas
* Scikit-learn
* Model Evaluation
* Confusion Matrix
* False Positive Rate (FPR)
* False Negative Rate (FNR)
* Fairness Metrics
* AI Ethics

